@inject IJSRuntime Js

@*
    The whole of one task, beside the list it came from.

    The order is this component's one decision, and it is the only reason it
    exists: what the thing is and where it has got to, then what it is filed
    under, then the facts you set on it, then the text. That reads outside-in —
    identity, classification, settings, content — so what a reader opened the
    panel to check is at the top, and what they came to write is the part with
    room to grow.

    The body is last because it is the only part with no natural height. Put it
    anywhere else and the detail rows land somewhere different on every task,
    which turns "set a due date" from a place you point at into something you
    hunt for.

    The heading line is the same three things a row in the list is: the circle
    that finishes it, the title, and the title being the thing you click to
    retitle it. A panel whose title was text while the row it opened from could
    be renamed in place would be a panel you have to close to fix a typo.
    Everything the row's rename decided holds here too — the draft is this
    component's, Enter settles it, Escape abandons it and puts the focus back on
    the title, leaving commits, and an empty or unchanged title raises nothing.

    Everything under the heading arrives as a slot. The panel says where tags,
    details and a body go; it does not say what a tag is, which rows a detail
    pane has, or whether the body is a list of sub-items or a page of markdown.
    That is the line every other component in this folder draws, and it is what
    lets one panel serve a checklist item, a prompt and a backlog entry rather
    than one of the three.

    The body has no label. What is in it is either plainly a list or plainly
    prose, and a word over it would be a word to keep true: "Steps" over a body
    that turned out to be a paragraph is worse than nothing over either.

    Two of those slots are for the parts a panel in a real product has and a
    storybook one does not. Filing is the line of badges and pickers that says what
    kind of thing this is and where it is filed — beside the tags, because they
    are the same question asked twice. Footer is what acts on the whole task
    rather than on one field of it: closing it, deleting it, showing its source.
    Both are drawn only when something is passed, like everything else here, so a
    panel that has neither is the panel this component started as.

    Footer is last for the reason the body is second to last: what acts on the
    whole task should not sit between the reader and the part of it they are
    writing. Filing is above the details rather than below the heading, because a
    reader who wanted the title got it in the first line, and one who wanted the
    filing wants the tags with it.

    The panel can be tabbed into and out of, which is not a decoration: a panel is
    beside a list, and reading order puts it after the whole of that list. A host
    that hands the keyboard over on Tab needs somewhere to hand it to, so the panel
    names its first control and offers a way back off the front of it — the same
    pair TaskItem carries, for the same reason. The ids are minted here rather than
    handed in, because unlike a row the panel is one instance the host already
    holds.

    Tags are the exception to the slots, and only just: the panel draws the chips
    because TagChip is what a tag looks like everywhere in the product and a slot
    here would be an invitation to draw a second kind. Which affordances those
    chips have is still the host's, said the way this library always says it — a
    chip is removable when somebody is listening for the removal, and clickable
    when somebody is listening for the click. Nobody listening means plain text,
    not a control that does nothing.

    No width. A side panel is as wide as the pane it is docked in, and a
    component that had an opinion about that would be one every host had to
    argue with.
*@

<aside class="@ClassValue" aria-labelledby="@_titleId" data-testid="@TestId">
    <header class="task-panel__header">
        @* The circle is a real checkbox to anything that is listening and a circle
           to everyone else, the same bargain a row in the list makes — and the same
           stylesheet rule draws it, so the two are one shape and not two that
           happen to match. Read-only when nobody is listening: a checkbox that
           silently does nothing is worse than no checkbox. *@
        @if (CanToggle)
        {
            <button type="button"
                    class="task-panel__check"
                    id="@_checkId"
                    role="checkbox"
                    aria-checked="@(Done ? "true" : "false")"
                    aria-label="@Title"
                    data-testid="@Suffix("check")"
                    @onkeydown="e => OnLeadingKeyAsync(e, leading: true)"
                    @onclick="() => OnToggle.InvokeAsync()">
                @if (Done)
                {
                    <span class="task-panel__tick" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
                }
            </button>
        }
        else if (Done)
        {
            <span class="task-panel__check"
                  role="img"
                  aria-label="Done"
                  data-testid="@Suffix("check")">
                <span class="task-panel__tick" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
            </span>
        }

        @* One heading, whatever state the title is in. The panel is named by this
           element, so replacing it with a field while somebody types would take the
           panel's name away mid-edit — and put a hole in the page's outline where
           its only heading was. *@
        <h2 class="task-panel__title" id="@_titleId">
            @if (_editing)
            {
                <TextField Value="@_draft"
                           ValueChanged="value => _draft = value"
                           AriaLabel="@($"Rename {Title}")"
                           Bare="true"
                           InputCssClass="task-panel__rename-input"
                           TestId="@Suffix("rename")"
                           Id="@_renameId"
                           @onkeydown="OnRenameKeyAsync"
                           @onblur="CommitRenameAsync" />
            }
            else if (CanRename)
            {
                @* The title is the trigger, and there is no pencil. A pencil beside
                   a heading this size would be a second target for the thing the
                   heading already is, and the heading is the biggest target on the
                   panel. *@
                <button type="button"
                        class="task-panel__title-button"
                        id="@_titleButtonId"
                        data-testid="@Suffix("title")"
                        @onkeydown="e => OnLeadingKeyAsync(e, leading: !CanToggle)"
                        @onclick="StartRenameAsync">@Title</button>
            }
            else
            {
                @Title
            }
        </h2>

        @* Beside the title rather than under it: a status is what the title is
           currently doing, and a reader who wants both wants them in one look. *@
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Status))
        {
            <StatusBadge Status="@Status" CssClass="task-panel__status" TestId="@Suffix("status")" />
        }
    </header>

    @if (TagList.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="task-panel__tags" data-testid="@Suffix("tags")">
            @foreach (var tag in TagList)
            {
                <TagChip Text="@tag"
                         Removable="@OnTagRemoved.HasDelegate"
                         OnRemove="OnTagRemoved"
                         OnClick="@TagClick(tag)" />
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* Beside the tags rather than under the heading: "what kind of thing is this
       and where is it filed" is the same question the chips answer, and a badge
       strip separated from the tags by a pane of settings would be two answers
       with the rows for something else in between. *@
    @if (Filing is not null)
    {
        <div class="task-panel__filing" data-testid="@Suffix("filing")">
            @Filing
        </div>
    }

    @if (Details is not null)
    {
        <div class="task-panel__details" data-testid="@Suffix("details")">
            @Details
        </div>
    }

    @if (Body is not null)
    {
        <div class="task-panel__body" data-testid="@Suffix("body")">
            @Body
        </div>
    }

    @* After the body, because what acts on the whole task must not stand between
       the reader and the part of it they came to write. *@
    @if (Footer is not null)
    {
        <div class="task-panel__footer" data-testid="@Suffix("footer")">
            @Footer
        </div>
    }
</aside>

@code {
    /// <summary>The task's title, and the panel's accessible name with it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Whether it is finished. Drives the circle and quietens the title,
    /// the same two things it does to a row.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Done { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Somebody pressed the circle. Without it the circle is state rather
    /// than a control — and on a task with nothing to tick, nothing at all.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnToggle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The title, retitled, once it settles. Without it the title is a
    /// heading and not a button.
    /// <para>
    /// The title alone, unlike <see cref="TaskRename"/>: a panel is one task, so
    /// there is no id to say which. Reported when it settles rather than per
    /// keystroke, because a rename that raised every character would put a save per
    /// character into whatever the host does with these — and would make Escape
    /// mean nothing, since the change it was meant to abandon had already landed.
    /// </para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnRename { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where it has got to, as the word to show — "Ready", "In
    /// progress". Already said: what a status is called, and in what language,
    /// belongs to the host, exactly as a due date does.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What it is filed under. Left off entirely when there is nothing
    /// — an empty tag strip is a row of blank space where the reader learns
    /// there is nothing to learn.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string>? Tags { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Somebody clicked a tag, meaning "show me its siblings". Without
    /// it the chips are text.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnTagSelected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Somebody dropped a tag. Without it the chips have no ✕:
    /// filtering by a tag and taking it off are different intents, so they are
    /// separately opted into.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnTagRemoved { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What kind of thing it is and where it is filed — the badges, selectors and
    /// links a product puts on a task.
    /// <para>
    /// A slot rather than parameters, because every one of those is the host's
    /// vocabulary: which statuses there are, whether a task belongs to a
    /// repository, what an issue link looks like. A panel that took a status
    /// <em>list</em> would be a panel that had answered a question this library
    /// does not get to answer — which is why <see cref="Status"/> is one already
    /// formatted word and not a picker.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Filing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The rows you set things on — <see cref="TaskActionPane"/> in
    /// practice.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Details { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The rest of the task: a list of sub-items, or its markdown. Both
    /// are the same slot because they are the same thing to the panel — the part
    /// that is as long as it is.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Body { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What acts on the whole task rather than on one field of it — close it,
    /// delete it, show where it is stored.
    /// <para>
    /// Last, and that is the whole of what this slot decides. These controls have
    /// to be somewhere, and every other place puts them between the reader and the
    /// body: above it they push the part with no natural height further down on
    /// every task, and beside the heading they sit next to the one control that
    /// looks like them and does the opposite.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Footer { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The reader tabbed backward off the front of the panel: the way back out of
    /// a panel a host tabbed them into.
    /// <para>
    /// Raised from the panel's <em>first</em> control only — the circle when there
    /// is one, the title when there is not. Raising it from the title as well would
    /// put the circle behind a Shift+Tab that no longer goes there, which is the
    /// same bargain <see cref="TaskItem.OnTabBackward"/> refuses for a row.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing is prevented, for the reason a row gives: Blazor resolves
    /// <c>preventDefault</c> when the component renders rather than when the key
    /// arrives, so suppressing Shift+Tab here would suppress Tab and Enter with it.
    /// The browser's own move still happens, and the focus a host sets afterwards
    /// is the one that lasts.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnTabBackward { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private readonly string _checkId = "task-panel-check-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    private readonly string _titleId = "task-panel-title-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    private readonly string _titleButtonId = "task-panel-title-button-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    private readonly string _renameId = "task-panel-rename-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    private bool _editing;
    private string _draft = string.Empty;
    private bool _focusRename;
    private bool _focusTitle;

    private IReadOnlyList<string> TagList => Tags ?? [];

    private bool CanToggle => OnToggle.HasDelegate;

    private bool CanRename => OnRename.HasDelegate;

    private string ClassValue => string.Join(' ', new[]
    {
        "task-panel",
        Done ? "task-panel--done" : null,
        CssClass
    }.Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part)));

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";

    /// <summary>Where <see cref="FocusFirstAsync"/> aims and which element raises
    /// <see cref="OnTabBackward"/>: the circle when the panel has one, the title
    /// when it does not. Null on a panel that is only a heading, in which case
    /// there is nothing here to focus.</summary>
    private string? FirstControlId => CanToggle ? _checkId : CanRename ? _titleButtonId : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the focus on the first control in the panel.
    /// <para>
    /// The counterpart to <see cref="OnTabBackward"/>. A host that hands the
    /// keyboard over to the panel — because reading order puts it after the whole
    /// list it is beside — needs somewhere to hand it to, and which control that is
    /// today is exactly what it should not have to know.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public Task FocusFirstAsync() =>
        FirstControlId is { } id ? QuickEditField.FocusAsync(Js, id, select: false) : Task.CompletedTask;

    /// <summary>Shift+Tab off the front of the panel, from the one control that is
    /// the front of it. <paramref name="leading"/> is false on the title whenever
    /// there is a circle before it, so the circle stays reachable.</summary>
    private async Task OnLeadingKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e, bool leading)
    {
        if (leading && e.Key is "Tab" && e.ShiftKey) await OnTabBackward.InvokeAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The chip's click, carrying the tag it was on. Default when
    /// nobody is listening, so TagChip renders the label as text rather than as
    /// a button that goes nowhere.</summary>
    private EventCallback<MouseEventArgs> TagClick(string tag) =>
        OnTagSelected.HasDelegate
            ? EventCallback.Factory.Create<MouseEventArgs>(this, () => OnTagSelected.InvokeAsync(tag))
            : default;

    private Task StartRenameAsync()
    {
        if (!CanRename) return Task.CompletedTask;

        _draft = Title;
        _editing = true;

        // Asked for here, done after the render: the field this describes is a node
        // some later batch creates, so there is nothing to focus at this point.
        _focusRename = true;

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    /// <summary>The caret in the field, or the ring back on the title, once each
    /// and after the render that made the element. Once is what the flags are for:
    /// a panel re-renders whenever anything in it changes, and a focus call on
    /// every render would drag the caret back over the title mid-sentence.</summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (_focusRename)
        {
            _focusRename = false;
            await QuickEditField.FocusAsync(Js, _renameId);
        }

        if (_focusTitle)
        {
            _focusTitle = false;
            await QuickEditField.FocusAsync(Js, _titleButtonId, select: false);
        }
    }

    private async Task OnRenameKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            // Abandoned: the heading goes back to what it said and nothing is
            // raised. The ring goes back to the title, because that is what a
            // dismissal owes its trigger — without it the focus lands on the
            // document and a keyboard reader starts the page again.
            _editing = false;
            _draft = string.Empty;
            _focusTitle = CanRename;
            return;
        }

        if (e.Key == "Enter") await CommitRenameAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>Settles the draft. An empty title is not a rename — a reader who
    /// cleared the field wants the title they had, not a task with no name — and
    /// neither is one that comes back the same.</summary>
    private async Task CommitRenameAsync()
    {
        if (!_editing) return;

        _editing = false;

        var title = _draft.Trim();
        _draft = string.Empty;

        if (title.Length == 0 || title == Title) return;

        await OnRename.InvokeAsync(title);
    }
}
